        "content": "Before drafting a formal project report, an entrepreneur must conduct a multi-dimensional feasibility study to determine whether the proposed enterprise should be pursued, modified, or abandoned.\n\n### The Four Pillars of Feasibility\n1. **Technical Feasibility:** Assessment of production technology, availability of certified planting material/raw materials, water and electrical load availability, plant capacity, labor skill availability, and environmental suitability of agro-climatic conditions.\n2. **Market Feasibility:** Evaluating total market demand, pricing flexibility, target customer segments, distribution logistics, competitor market share, and barrier-to-entry dynamics.\n3. **Financial Feasibility:** Rigorous appraisal of capital cost, working capital cycles, projected operating cash flows, Net Present Value (NPV), Internal Rate of Return (IRR), Debt Service Coverage Ratio (DSCR), and Break-Even Point (BEP).\n4. **Environmental and Legal Feasibility:** Compliance with state pollution control boards (Consent to Establish/Operate), EIA (Environmental Impact Assessment), water extraction limits, and pesticide regulatory approvals.",
