      "modelAnswer": "A Business Plan is a comprehensive, formal strategic document that defines the operational identity, mission, target market, competitive positioning, management structure, and multi-year financial projections of a proposed or expanding business enterprise.\n\nPrimary Functions of an Agribusiness Plan:\n\n1. Internal Operational Functions:\n- Strategic Compass and Operating Roadmap: Translates broad visionary goals into actionable, time-bound milestones, coordinating procurement, processing, logistics, and sales.\n- Resource Allocation and Cash Discipline: Details monthly cash flows, inventory holding periods, and capital expenditures, preventing unplanned cash burns during off-season cycles.\n- Performance Benchmark: Establishes quantitative Key Performance Indicators (KPIs) against which actual field yields, factory utilization, and sales revenues are evaluated.\n\n2. External Financing and Stakeholder Functions:\n- Credit Appraisal Vehicle: Serves as the mandatory basis on which commercial banks, Regional Rural Banks, and development institutions (NABARD, SFAC) evaluate project feasibility and Debt Service Coverage Ratio (DSCR) before sanctioning loans.\n- Investor Pitch Deck Foundation: Conveys commercial viability, unit economics, and return on investment (ROI) to angel investors, venture capital funds, and grant screening committees (RKVY-RAFTAAR).\n- Regulatory and Partnership Compliance: Demonstrates adherence to environmental, zoning, and food safety standards (FSSAI), instilling confidence in institutional buyers and corporate retail off-takers.",
