      "questionText": "Explain the four quadrants of a SWOT Analysis with concrete examples from a commercial mushroom production venture.",
      "modelAnswer": "SWOT Analysis is a foundational strategic planning framework that evaluates the internal Strengths and Weaknesses of an enterprise alongside the external Opportunities and Threats present in the market environment.\n\nApplication to a Commercial Mushroom Production Venture (Button & Oyster):\n\n1. Strengths (Internal Positive Attributes):\n- Abundant and Low-Cost Raw Material: Location in a major wheat/paddy belt provides direct access to cheap straw for compost formulation.\n- Technical Mastery: Founder holds an accredited B.Sc. (Hons.) Agriculture degree with specialized practical training in mushroom spawn production and environmental climate control.\n\n2. Weaknesses (Internal Constraints):\n- High Initial Capital Expenditure: Heavy capital required to install insulated growing chambers, climate-controlled chillers, and automated humidifiers.\n- Perishability and Cold Chain Deficit: Fresh mushrooms have a post-harvest shelf life of barely 48 hours without refrigeration, exposing the firm to rapid spoilage if transit breaks down.\n\n3. Opportunities (External Favorable Trends):\n- Surging Urban Health and Protein Demand: Growing consumer preference for vegetarian protein, dietary fiber, and medicinal mushrooms (Cordyceps, Ganoderma) in nearby tier-1 cities.\n- Government Subsidies: Availability of 40-50% capital subsidies for establishing integrated mushroom spawn and cultivation units under the Mission for Integrated Development of Horticulture (MIDH).\n\n4. Threats (External Challenges):\n- Power Tariff and Supply Volatility: Frequent rural electrical load-shedding forcing costly reliance on diesel generators to run air conditioners.\n- Fluctuations in Spawn Quality and Disease Outbreaks: Contamination risks from green mold (*Trichoderma*) or bacterial blotch wiping out cropping beds rapidly.",
