      "difficulty": "Medium",
      "syllabusTopic": "Feasibility analysis: technical, market, financial, and environmental feasibility",
      "questionText": "Explain the Debt Service Coverage Ratio (DSCR). Why do commercial lending institutions insist on a DSCR between 1.5 and 2.0 for agricultural projects?",
      "modelAnswer": "The Debt Service Coverage Ratio (DSCR) is the paramount financial ratio utilized by commercial banks, Regional Rural Banks, and financial institutions to measure a borrower's operational ability to service their annual term loan commitments (interest charges plus principal repayments).\n\n1. Mathematical Formula:\n$$\\text{DSCR} = \\frac{\\text{Net Profit After Tax} + \\text{Depreciation} + \\text{Interest on Term Loan}}{\\text{Interest on Term Loan} + \\text{Principal Repayment Installment}}$$\n*Note: Depreciation is added back because it is a non-cash expense; interest on term loan is added back to evaluate total cash generated before debt servicing.*\n\n2. Why Banks Mandate a DSCR Between 1.5 and 2.0 for Agribusiness:\n- The 1.0 Baseline: A DSCR of exactly 1.0 means the enterprise generates just enough cash flow to service its debt with zero margin of error. A DSCR below 1.0 represents immediate cash default.\n- Biological and Climate Buffer: Agribusiness is inherently vulnerable to unpredictable external shocks\u2014erratic monsoons, sudden pest epidemics, sharp commodity price collapses, and unseasonal rains.\n- Safety Cushion: A DSCR of 1.75 means that even if operating cash flow declines by 40% due to an unexpected crop yield dip or input price surge, the business still generates sufficient net cash to pay its bank installments on time without defaulting.\n- Working Capital Re-investment: A healthy DSCR ensures the enterprise retains enough residual cash after loan repayment to fund expanding seasonal working capital needs.",
      "outlinePoints": [
        "Definition of DSCR as the key debt-servicing capability indicator",
        "Full mathematical formula showing add-back of non-cash depreciation and interest",
        "Interpretation of DSCR values (<1.0 default, 1.0 zero buffer)",
        "Need for 1.5 to 2.0 buffer in agriculture due to climatic and price volatility",
        "Protection of bank depositor capital and retained liquidity for working capital"
      ]
