      "syllabusTopic": "Business Model Canvas (BMC) and value proposition design for agri-ventures",
      "questionText": "List the nine building blocks of Alexander Osterwalder's Business Model Canvas (BMC). Briefly state what each block addresses.",
      "modelAnswer": "Alexander Osterwalder and Yves Pigneur designed the Business Model Canvas (BMC) as a visual, strategic one-page blueprint that encapsulates how an organization creates, delivers, and captures commercial value across nine fundamental building blocks:\n\n1. Customer Segments (CS): Defines the specific groups of consumers, businesses, or institutions the venture targets (e.g., urban direct-to-consumer health buyers, B2B wholesale exporters).\n2. Value Propositions (VP): The unique bundle of products, services, and benefits that solve customer problems or fulfill unmet needs (e.g., certified pesticide-free vegetables delivered within 12 hours of harvest).\n3. Channels (CH): The communication, distribution, and retail touchpoints through which the value proposition reaches customers (e.g., mobile app, rural kiosks, cold-chain retail).\n4. Customer Relationships (CR): The style and mechanism of relationship established with each customer segment (e.g., personal agronomic advisory, automated digital subscriptions).\n5. Revenue Streams (RS): The cash flows generated from customer value exchange (e.g., direct crop sales, machinery rental fees, software licensing).\n6. Key Resources (KR): The vital physical, financial, intellectual, and human assets required to execute the model (e.g., sorting-grading machinery, agronomists, proprietary seed germplasm).\n7. Key Activities (KA): The most critical operational actions the firm must execute (e.g., soil health testing, cold-chain transport, FSSAI quality testing).\n8. Key Partnerships (KP): The network of external suppliers, FPOs, research institutes (ICAR), and logistics providers that optimize operations and reduce risk.\n9. Cost Structure (CS): The major operational and capital monetary expenditures incurred to operate the business (e.g., raw material procurement, power, logistics, salaries).",
