      "modelAnswer": "A technical feasibility study investigates whether the engineering, biological, and physical parameters of a proposed fruit processing and packaging facility are sound, robust, and capable of operating profitably.\n\nKey Technical Parameters Evaluated:\n\n1. Plant Location and Raw Material Proximity:\n- Raw material availability within an economic radius (typically 30-50 km) to minimize freight costs and transit bruising of perishable fruits (e.g., mangoes, guavas).\n- Seasonal availability calendars mapping crop harvesting windows to calculate required storage volumes.\n\n2. Plant Capacity and Production Scheduling:\n- Deciding rated capacity (e.g., 5 MT/hour fruit washing, pulping, and aseptic packaging line).\n- Modeling phased capacity utilization ramp-up (Year 1: 50%, Year 2: 70%, Year 3: 85%) to account for commissioning trials.\n\n3. Process Technology and Machinery Selection:\n- Selection of processing equipment: continuous rotary washers, fruit pulpers, decanter centrifuges, tubular pasteurizers, and aseptic bag-in-box filling lines.\n- Sourcing machinery from reputable fabricators with readily accessible spare parts.\n\n4. Infrastructure and Utility Requirements:\n- Water Quality and Quantity: Availability of potable industrial water matching FSSAI standards (often 5 to 10 liters of water per kg of fruit processed).\n- Electric Power Load: Sanctioned HT industrial electrical connection with backup diesel generators or rooftop solar installations to avoid processing interruptions.\n\n5. Effluent Treatment and Environmental Compliance:\n- Design of an Effluent Treatment Plant (ETP) capable of handling high Biological Oxygen Demand (BOD) and Chemical Oxygen Demand (COD) waste from fruit washings and peels to comply with State Pollution Control Board norms.",
