      "questionText": "What are the essential financial statements included in a bankable Detailed Project Report (DPR)?",
      "modelAnswer": "A bankable Detailed Project Report (DPR) submitted to commercial banks and development institutions must include four interconnected pro-forma financial schedules projected over a 5 to 7 year operational horizon:\n\n1. Cost of Project and Means of Finance:\n- Cost of Project: Itemizes capital expenditures including land acquisition, site development, factory civil works, plant and machinery, electrification, pre-operative expenses, and working capital margin.\n- Means of Finance: Explains how these costs will be funded, specifying promoter equity contribution, bank term loans, and government capital subsidies.\n\n2. Projected Profit and Loss Account (Income Statement):\n- Details projected revenues from sales, deducting cost of raw materials, utilities, factory labor, administrative overheads, sales commissions, depreciation, and bank interest to determine Operating Profit (EBIT), Profit Before Tax (PBT), and Net Profit After Tax (PAT).\n\n3. Projected Balance Sheet:\n- Presents the enterprise\u2019s financial health at the end of each financial year, balancing Total Liabilities (promoter equity, accumulated reserves, term loans, bank working capital borrowings, sundry creditors) with Total Assets (net fixed assets, current inventory, accounts receivable, cash balances).\n\n4. Projected Cash Flow Statement:\n- Maps actual monthly and annual cash movements categorized into Operating, Investing, and Financing activities. Banks evaluate this statement to verify that cash generation is consistently positive and sufficient to service debt.",
