      "difficulty": "Medium",
      "syllabusTopic": "Meaning, significance, and key components of a business plan",
      "questionText": "Detail the Operational and Technical Plan section of an Agribusiness Plan for setting up a 10,000 Liters/Day Commercial Dairy Processing and Packaging Enterprise.",
      "modelAnswer": "The Operational and Technical Plan forms the physical engine of a business plan, demonstrating to financiers that the founders possess the engineering, supply chain, and logistical capabilities to convert raw milk into marketable packaged dairy products.\n\nOPERATIONAL AND TECHNICAL PLAN: 10,000 LPD DAIRY ENTERPRISE\n\n1. Raw Milk Procurement and Supply Chain Network:\n- Village Milk Collection Centres (MCCs): Establishing 20 automated collection centers within a 25 km radius. Each MCC is equipped with an electronic milk analyzer (testing FAT and SNF in 30 seconds), digital weighing scale, and immediate solar milk chiller (Bulk Milk Coolers - BMC of 1,000L capacity).\n- Logistics: Insulated stainless-steel road tankers (SS 304) collect chilled milk (at 4\u00b0C) twice daily, ensuring arrival at the main dairy plant with bacteriological methylene blue reduction time (MBRT) exceeding 4 hours.\n\n2. Plant Location, Civil Layout, and Zoning:\n- Site: 2-acre plot in an approved agro-industrial cluster with proximity to state highways, uninterrupted 3-phase HT power, and deep borewell water.\n- Layout Design: Segregated hygiene zones adhering to Codex Alimentarius:\n  - Raw Milk Reception Dock (RMRD)\n  - Processing and Homogenization Hall\n  - Cold Storage Room (maintained at 2-4\u00b0C)\n  - Boiler and Refrigeration Utility House\n  - Effluent Treatment Plant (ETP) positioned downwind.\n\n3. Processing Technology and Equipment Selection:\n- Capacity: 10,000 Liters per Day (LPD) operational on an 8-hour shift, expandable to 20,000 LPD.\n- Core Equipment:\n  - Plate Pasteurizer (High-Temperature Short-Time - HTST: 72\u00b0C for 15 seconds) with automated flow diversion valve.\n  - Centrifugal Cream Separator (tri-process: clarifying, standardizing, separating).\n  - High-Pressure Homogenizer (operating at 2,000 PSI) for uniform fat dispersion.\n  - Clean-In-Place (CIP) automated chemical sanitation system.\n  - Automated Form-Fill-Seal (FFS) pouch packaging machines for liquid milk, and paneer presses/vacuum packaging units.\n\n4. Utilities and Quality Assurance:\n- Refrigeration: Ammonia/Freon vapor compression refrigeration system with an ice bank tank (IBT) providing ice water for chilling.\n- Steam Generation: Automated briquette-fired boiler (600 kg/hr steam output) utilizing agricultural biomass briquettes.\n- Laboratory: Fully equipped microbiology and chemistry lab for testing aflatoxin M1, antibiotic residues, microbial plate count, and adulterants.\n\n5. Product Mix and Mass Balance:\n- Standardized Toned Milk (3.0% Fat, 8.5% SNF): 6,500 LPD in 500 ml pouches.\n- Full Cream Milk (6.0% Fat, 9.0% SNF): 2,000 LPD.\n- Value-Added Fresh Paneer: 150 kg/day (utilizing 1,200 L milk).\n- Ghee: 30 kg/day manufactured from surplus standardized cream.",
