      "syllabusTopic": "Detailed Project Report (DPR) preparation: format and guidelines",
      "questionText": "Compare a Pre-Feasibility Report and a Detailed Project Report (DPR) in terms of purpose, depth, cost, and decision-making utility.",
      "modelAnswer": "Entrepreneurs navigate two distinct analytical stages when transforming an enterprise concept into a funded reality:\n\n1. Primary Purpose:\n- Pre-Feasibility Report: An early-stage exploratory document designed to determine whether a project concept warrants further investment of time and money, filtering out unviable ideas before heavy capital commitment.\n- Detailed Project Report (DPR): An exhaustive, bankable engineering, financial, and operational master document submitted to commercial banks, boards of directors, and government bodies for formal loan sanction and project execution.\n\n2. Depth of Data and Precision:\n- Pre-Feasibility Report: Relies predominantly on secondary research, broad industry averages, rough cost-per-square-foot benchmarks, and tentative market estimates (error margin \u00b115-20%).\n- Detailed Project Report: Based on rigorous primary surveys, certified vendor quotations for machinery, architectural civil blueprints, soil load-bearing tests, and multi-year projected financial schedules (error margin within \u00b15%).\n\n3. Preparation Cost and Time:\n- Pre-Feasibility Report: Prepared rapidly (2 to 4 weeks) at low cost, often formulated internally by the founding team.\n- Detailed Project Report: Requires 2 to 6 months of detailed engineering, financial modeling, and regulatory vetting by specialized technical consultants and chartered accountants at substantial cost.\n\n4. Decision-Making Utility:\n- Pre-Feasibility Report: Serves an internal 'Go / No-Go' screening purpose for the entrepreneur.\n- Detailed Project Report: Serves as the legally binding basis for commercial bank loan sanctions, term debt disbursements, and contractor bidding.",
