      "syllabusTopic": "Feasibility analysis: technical, market, financial, and environmental feasibility",
      "questionText": "Compare Technical Feasibility and Market Feasibility. Why must both be simultaneously favorable for project viability?",
      "modelAnswer": "Technical Feasibility and Market Feasibility represent the two foundational pillars of pre-investment appraisal:\n\n1. Core Investigation Question:\n- Technical Feasibility: Asks 'Can we make it?' It investigates whether the product can be manufactured with the available technology, raw materials, labor, agro-climatic conditions, and utilities within cost parameters.\n- Market Feasibility: Asks 'Can we sell it at a profit?' It investigates whether sufficient customer demand exists, what price customers are willing to pay, who the competitors are, and how distribution channels will be accessed.\n\n2. Primary Analytical Variables:\n- Technical Feasibility: Plant capacity, conversion ratios, material balances, machine specifications, raw material seasonality, water quality, electric power load, and effluent disposal.\n- Market Feasibility: Total Addressable Market (TAM), customer demographics, price elasticity, substitute products, competitor market share, advertising strategies, and distributor margins.\n\n3. Failure Modes:\n- Technical Failure: The firm discovers that raw mangoes rot during storage, power outages burn motors, or chemical extraction fails, resulting in zero salable output.\n- Market Failure: The firm successfully manufactures top-quality mango puree, but finds no buyers because existing multinational beverage brands have locked up all long-term distributor contracts.\n\n4. Why Simultaneous Favorability is Mandatory:\nCommercial viability is a multiplicative function (Viability = Technical Feasibility \u00d7 Market Feasibility). If either variable is zero, total project viability collapses to zero:\n- A technically flawless product with zero market demand results in warehouses full of unsalable, decaying inventory and financial bankruptcy.\n- A massive market demand for an agricultural product with unresolved technical hurdles (e.g., inability to maintain sterility in tissue culture) results in contract failure, unfulfilled orders, and legal liabilities.\n\nTherefore, an entrepreneur must establish both technical capability and market demand simultaneously before committing capital.",
      "outlinePoints": [
        "Core question: 'Can we engineer and manufacture it?' vs 'Can we sell it at a profitable price?'",
        "Analytical parameters: Engineering, utilities, material balances vs TAM, pricing, competitor dynamics",
        "Failure modes: Production collapse vs Commercial demand deficit",
        "The multiplicative principle: Why positive technical feasibility is useless without market demand and vice versa",
        "Synthesis proving that both must be favorable before committing irreversible capital"
      ]
