      "id": "AEE201-U3-SUB19",
      "questionType": "APPLICATION",
      "maxMarks": 8,
      "difficulty": "Hard",
      "syllabusTopic": "Project identification, ideation, and opportunity screening",
      "questionText": "Design a comprehensive Opportunity Screening Matrix for a state agricultural incubation centre to evaluate and rank 50 startup applicant proposals, covering technical, market, regulatory, financial, and environmental filters.",
      "modelAnswer": "A multi-dimensional Opportunity Screening Matrix enables an agribusiness incubator to objectively evaluate and score startup proposals, filtering viable innovations from high-risk ventures:\n\nTHE 100-POINT MULTI-CRITERIA OPPORTUNITY SCREENING MATRIX:\n\n1. Dimension 1: Market Attractiveness and Problem Urgency (Weight: 25 Points)\n- Size of Addressable Market (TAM/SAM): Large, expanding target market (>\u20b9500 Cr national or >\u20b950 Cr regional) (8 pts)\n- Severity of Rural Pain Point: Solves an acute agricultural problem (e.g. 30% crop loss, severe groundwater depletion) vs minor convenience (9 pts)\n- Competitive Advantage & Defensibility: Clear unique value proposition, high switching costs, or proprietary technology (8 pts)\n\n2. Dimension 2: Technical and Operational Feasibility (Weight: 20 Points)\n- Technology Readiness Level (TRL): Working physical prototype validated in field trials (TRL 5-7) vs raw theoretical idea (TRL 1-2) (8 pts)\n- Raw Material Supply Chain Security: Year-round availability, low vulnerability to localized crop failure (6 pts)\n- Scalability of Production: Model can be replicated across diverse agro-climatic zones with modular engineering (6 pts)\n\n3. Dimension 3: Financial Viability and Unit Economics (Weight: 20 Points)\n- Gross Margin and Contribution: Strong gross margin (>35-40%) capable of absorbing seasonal input price spikes (7 pts)\n- Projected Return Metrics: Estimated IRR > 22%, positive NPV at 12% hurdle, and payback < 4 years (7 pts)\n- Capital Efficiency: Clear capital budgeting roadmap with manageable working capital cycles (6 pts)\n\n4. Dimension 4: Regulatory, Environmental, and Social Impact (Weight: 15 Points)\n- Regulatory Clarity: Fast-track statutory clearance (FSSAI, CIB&RC, organic certification) with zero legal bans (5 pts)\n- Environmental Sustainability: Net positive ecological footprint (water-saving, zero chemical residues, soil carbon enrichment) (5 pts)\n- Smallholder Inclusivity: Demonstrable income enhancement (>15-20%) for participating smallholders (5 pts)\n\n5. Dimension 5: Founder Pedigree and Execution Capability (Weight: 20 Points)\n- Domain Expertise: Complementary team possessing agricultural technical knowledge paired with commercial marketing skills (8 pts)\n- McClelland's Entrepreneurial Drive: High achievement motivation (n-Ach), persistence, and calculated risk propensity (7 pts)\n- Coachability and Integrity: Willingness to accept mentor feedback, adapt hypotheses, and uphold ethical governance (5 pts)\n\nDECISION RUBRIC:\n- Score \u2265 75: Select for Direct Induction into High-Support Cohort with Seed Capital Linkage.\n- Score 60 - 74: Conditional Pre-Incubation (3-month prototype refinement and mentorship).\n- Score < 60: Reject with detailed diagnostic feedback on critical feasibility gaps.",
